[{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2633872474","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/102360#issuecomment-2633872474","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/102360","id":2633872474,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc6c_bRa","user":{"login":"berarma","id":162766,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2Mjc2Ng==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/162766?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma","html_url":"https://github.com/berarma","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-02-04T13:15:21Z","updated_at":"2025-02-11T10:42:01Z","body":"I've removed a short silence after a seek that was intentional to allow for the audio to sync without having to start decoding audio sooner. It was usually unnoticeable, but I found some files where the silence could be up to 1s long. Now, it will play audio from the very start without any silence.\r\n\r\nWhile working on this, I thought I would explain a bit about how I've implemented the seek function in case anyone is interested. My goal was performance and precision, and at the same time make the code as simple as possible.\r\n\r\n# Long technical explanation\r\n\r\nThe only official documentation I could find, apart from the API ref, was [this wiki page](https://wiki.xiph.org/GranulePosAndSeeking), but it's pretty vague about the details. I've found some more information and code that helped, but it was either too vague or too confusing for me to fully use, so I started my own implementation. Any code I'd used would have to be heavily adapted anyway.\r\n\r\nOGG doesn't use indexes. The basic storage unit is a page, and it uses granules to put time marks on pages. Granulepos is the time unit in OGG containers. For video streams, this granulepos can be easily decoded into a frame number, or a key-frame number and an inter-frame offset. The problem is that not all pages have a granulepos, and when they have one, it's the granulepos of the last complete packet in the page. So finding the granule we need isn't as easy as it may seem, and it involves scanning through several pages until we guess the page it is in.\r\n\r\nSomething similar happens with audio streams, although granulepos is calculated differently. In this case, it's easier because there are no key-frames or alike.\r\n\r\nBut in both cases, when scanning, we have to make sure we catch the page where the packet we want starts.\r\n\r\nThere's an added complication for video. Calculating the key-frame and the inter-frame offset from a granulepos is easy, but the inverse operation isn't. The GOP inside a stream is variable, although most streams don't change it. We only know the max GOP for a stream, so it's impossible to calculate the granulepos from a key-frame number.\r\n\r\n~~And one more glitch to account for. I've found at least one file that used duplicate granulepos instead of leaving it blank, so I had to filter this out.~~\r\n\r\nThe algorithm starts by guessing a position in the file where the key frame we're looking for might be, using the frame duration, the size in bytes of the file, and the stream length in seconds. This gives us a pretty good approximation. Then it starts scanning for the first page, and if we're past the time mark, it backtracks in the file.\r\n\r\nThe first page with a granulepos just before  our calculated granulepos key frame guess is the point where we start decoding. The initial decoding process is done as fast as possible up to the time we're seeking, then resume the normal decoding process.\r\n\r\nThis algorithm gives very short seek times on my computer (Ryzen 3600) with debug builds. Immediate in some videos and one or two tenths of a second in others. I have to try it yet on optimized builds. It will depend on the GOP used, the higher, the more frames it will have to decode before displaying the first frame.\r\n\r\n~~It could be a bit more optimized by making one more pass to look for the last key frame before decoding. This could improve seek times for streams with lower GOP values. We could also reduce backtracking in the file by fine-tuning the method or by using another method, although I don't think there's much to gain. As it is now, normally, one or too seeks in the file are enough, maxing at 4 or 5 seeks. In both cases, i think the added complexity might not pay back.~~ Edit: After a lot of testing and some improvements I don't think there's much more to gain.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2633872474/reactions","total_count":5,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":4,"rocket":1,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2648307126","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/102360#issuecomment-2648307126","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/102360","id":2648307126,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc6d2fW2","user":{"login":"berarma","id":162766,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2Mjc2Ng==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/162766?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma","html_url":"https://github.com/berarma","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-02-10T15:07:35Z","updated_at":"2025-02-10T15:07:35Z","body":"I've removed a workaround for a bad video encoding case that I only found in a very old video pre-dating Theora 1.0. It's not worth it.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2648307126/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2655743579","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/102360#issuecomment-2655743579","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/102360","id":2655743579,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc6eS25b","user":{"login":"berarma","id":162766,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE2Mjc2Ng==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/162766?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma","html_url":"https://github.com/berarma","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/berarma/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2025-02-13T07:30:25Z","updated_at":"2025-03-03T12:58:00Z","body":"> Tested locally using https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Big_Buck_Bunny_first_23_seconds_1080p.ogv and https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Big_Buck_Bunny_medium.ogv, it works as expected. I made sure sound remains in sync after seeking back and forth.\r\n> \r\n> I don't have an OGV file with more than 2 audio channels to test the downmixing functionality though.\r\n\r\n[This comment](godotengine/godot/issues/37536#issuecomment-2053667996) has a video with 7.1 audio. I've also tested with [this video](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YE7VzlLtp-4) that has 5.1 audio but it needs to be extracted and converted.\r\n\r\nEDIT: I've [uploaded the 5.1 video](https://www.mediafire.com/file/pttvxgo5vgovv8v/Big+Buck+Bunny.512.ogv/file) to make it easier.","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2655743579/reactions","total_count":3,"+1":3,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"minimized":null},{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/comments/2684459159","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/102360#issuecomment-2684459159","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/issues/102360","id":2684459159,"node_id":"IC_kwDOAO6SJc6gAZiX","user":{"login":"baldierot","id":174937294,"node_id":"U_kgDOCm1Uzg","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/174937294?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/baldierot","html_url":"https: